+
+## F-7 — Medium — Re-approving on every deposit is fragile with USDT
+
+**`src/UsdtYieldVault.sol:94`**
+
+Beyond returning no value (F-1), mainnet USDT refuses to overwrite a non-zero
+allowance with another non-zero value — `TetherToken.approve` contains
+`require(!((_value != 0) && (allowed[msg.sender][_spender] != 0)))`.
+
+`deposit` re-approves unconditionally on every call. Today Aave's `supply` consumes the
+full allowance, so it happens to be zero each time. But this is a latent trap: any
+future path that leaves even 1 unit of allowance behind — a partial supply, a different
+pool version, an Aave upgrade — bricks every subsequent deposit permanently.
+
+### Evidence
+
+```
+$ forge test --match-test test_Fork_RealUsdtRejectsNonZeroToNonZeroApprove -vv
+
+Ran 1 test for test/fork/MainnetForkIntegration.t.sol:MainnetForkIntegrationTest
+[PASS] test_Fork_RealUsdtRejectsNonZeroToNonZeroApprove() (gas: 39155)
+Suite result: ok. 1 passed; 0 failed; 0 skipped; finished in 355.99ms
+```
+
+This one **passes** — it asserts that real USDT rejects the second approve, confirming
+the constraint exists against the live token.
+
+### Fix
+
+Same one-line change as F-1: `usdt.forceApprove(address(aavePool), amount);`.
+`forceApprove` zeroes the allowance first, so the rule is never hit.
+
+---
+
+## F-8 — Low — `aUsdt` is stored and never read
+
+**`src/UsdtYieldVault.sol:41, 76`**
+
+Shown under F-2: `aUsdt` appears only in its declaration and its assignment. Nothing
+validates that the address passed actually is the aToken for USDT in that pool. Deploy
+with a typo'd `_aUsdt` today and nothing anywhere notices — but the moment you adopt
+F-2's fix, that same typo silently misprices every share in the vault.
+
+The constructor also does not reject `address(0)` for any of `_usdt`, `_aUsdt`,
+`_aavePool`, and does not bound `_depositFeeBps` (F-6 applies at construction too).
+
+### Fix
+
+Validate in the constructor, and cross-check against the pool rather than trusting the
+deployer:
+
+```solidity
+require(_usdt != address(0) && _aUsdt != address(0) && _aavePool != address(0));
+require(IAavePool(_aavePool).getReserveData(_usdt).aTokenAddress == _aUsdt, "aToken mismatch");
+require(_depositFeeBps <= MAX_DEPOSIT_FEE_BPS);
+```
+
+(`getReserveData` needs adding to `IAavePool`. I confirmed against the live pool that
+the USDT reserve's `aTokenAddress` is `0x23878914EFE38d27C4D67Ab83ed1b93A74D4086a`.)
+
+---
+
+## F-9 — Info — The "deposit fee" is not a protocol fee
+
+Worth stating plainly because the naming misleads, and because F-3 is built on it.
+
+The deposit fee is never collected by anyone. It is an instant wealth transfer from
+the incoming depositor to whoever already holds shares:
+
+- For the **first** depositor it is exactly zero, since she owns 100% of the shares it
+  accrues to. `test_Characterise_FirstDepositorPaysNoEffectiveFee` confirms she keeps
+  the full 100,000 despite a nominal 100 USDT fee.
+- For **later** depositors it is a real, immediate loss.
+  `test_Characterise_DepositFeeIsPaidToIncumbentHolders` shows Bob deposit 100,000 and
+  own 99,949.97 the same instant — 50.03 USDT gone, matching Alice's 50.03 gain.
+
+Both of these **pass** — I am documenting the behaviour, not alleging a defect. But it
+is not what "deposit fee" implies to a depositor or to whoever writes the marketing
+page, and it is the mechanism F-3 exploits at scale.
+
+---
+
+# What I would do before Monday
+
+Blocking, in order:
+
+1. **F-1** — `forceApprove`. One line, and without it nothing else matters.
+2. **F-2** — price shares off `aUsdt.balanceOf(address(this))`, with inflation-attack
+   mitigation. This is a real redesign, not a patch. Strongly consider OZ `ERC4626`.
+3. **F-3 / F-6** — cap the fee, add `minSharesOut`, timelock the owner.
+4. **F-5** — `withdraw` must not be pausable; disable `renounceOwnership`.
+5. **F-4** — decide where the exit fee goes and make the code do that.
+
+And on the test suite itself, since that is what let this through: the mocks need to
+lie less (a `approve`-returns-nothing USDT, an aToken that accrues), the four
+`assertGt(x, 0)` assertions need real expected values, and the fork/fuzz/invariant
+tests in this review should become part of CI rather than a one-off audit artifact.
+
+My honest read: F-1 is an afternoon. F-2 is a rewrite of the share-accounting core plus
+a fresh audit of the inflation-attack surface it opens. I do not think that lands
+safely by Monday.
diff --git a/test/AdminRisk.t.sol b/test/AdminRisk.t.sol
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..71d6225
--- /dev/null
+++ b/test/AdminRisk.t.sol
@@ -0,0 +1,132 @@
+// SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT
+pragma solidity 0.8.28;
+
+import {Test, console} from "forge-std/Test.sol";
+import {Pausable} from "@openzeppelin/contracts/utils/Pausable.sol";
+
+import {UsdtYieldVault} from "../src/UsdtYieldVault.sol";
+import {MockUSDT} from "./mocks/MockUSDT.sol";
+import {MockAavePool, MockAToken} from "./mocks/MockAavePool.sol";
+
+/// @notice What the owner key can do to depositors. The shipped suite tests only that
+///         non-owners are rejected; it never tests what a (possibly compromised) owner
+///         is permitted to do.
+contract AdminRiskTest is Test {
+    UsdtYieldVault vault;
+    MockUSDT usdt;
+    MockAavePool pool;
+    MockAToken aUsdt;
+
+    address owner = makeAddr("owner");
+    address alice = makeAddr("alice");
+
+    function setUp() public {
+        usdt = new MockUSDT();
+        pool = new MockAavePool(address(usdt));
+        aUsdt = pool.aToken();
+        vault = new UsdtYieldVault(address(usdt), address(aUsdt), address(pool), 10, owner);
+
+        usdt.mint(alice, 1_000_000e6);
+        usdt.mint(owner, 1_000_000e6);
+        vm.prank(alice);
+        usdt.approve(address(vault), type(uint256).max);
+        vm.prank(owner);
+        usdt.approve(address(vault), type(uint256).max);
+    }
+
+    /*//////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////
+        A-1: the deposit fee is unbounded
+    //////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////*/
+
+    /// @notice `setDepositFee` has no upper bound (src/UsdtYieldVault.sol:126-130).
+    ///         A fee over 100% is nonsense and should be rejected outright.
+    function test_SetDepositFeeRejectsFeesAbove100Percent() public {
+        vm.prank(owner);
+        vm.expectRevert();
+        vault.setDepositFee(10_001);
+    }
+
+    /// @notice And a >100% fee does not merely misprice -- `amount - fee` underflows,
+    ///         so deposits revert with a panic until the owner reverses it.
+    function test_DepositStillWorksAfterAnAbsurdFeeIsSet() public {
+        vm.prank(owner);
+        vault.setDepositFee(10_001);
+
+        vm.prank(alice);
+        vault.deposit(1_000e6);
+    }
+
+    /*//////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////
+        A-2: the owner can seize an incoming deposit
+    //////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////*/
+
+    /// @notice `deposit` takes no minimum-shares-out argument, and `setDepositFee`
+    ///         takes effect immediately. The owner can therefore front-run a deposit,
+    ///         spike the fee, and capture the difference through his own shares --
+    ///         the fee accrues to incumbent holders, and he is one.
+    function test_OwnerCannotSeizeAnIncomingDepositByFrontRunningTheFee() public {
+        // Owner holds a small position.
+        vm.prank(owner);
+        vault.deposit(1_000e6);
+        uint256 ownerBefore = vault.balanceOfUnderlying(owner);
+
+        // Alice's 100,000 USDT deposit is in the mempool. The owner front-runs it.
+        vm.prank(owner);
+        vault.setDepositFee(9_900); // 99%
+
+        vm.prank(alice);
+        vault.deposit(100_000e6);
+
+        uint256 ownerAfter = vault.balanceOfUnderlying(owner);
+        uint256 aliceAfter = vault.balanceOfUnderlying(alice);
+
+        console.log("owner stake before :", ownerBefore);
+        console.log("owner stake after  :", ownerAfter);
+        console.log("owner profit       :", ownerAfter - ownerBefore);
+        console.log("alice paid in      :", uint256(100_000e6));
+        console.log("alice now owns     :", aliceAfter);
+        console.log("alice's loss       :", 100_000e6 - aliceAfter);
+
+        assertApproxEqRel(aliceAfter, 100_000e6, 0.01e18, "a depositor must receive ~what she paid in");
+    }
+
+    /*//////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////
+        A-3: pause is a one-way door for depositors
+    //////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////*/
+
+    /// @notice `withdraw` is `whenNotPaused` (src/UsdtYieldVault.sol:107) and there is
+    ///         no emergency-exit path. Pausing freezes user principal, it does not
+    ///         protect it.
+    function test_PausedVaultStillLetsDepositorsExit() public {
+        vm.prank(alice);
+        uint256 shares = vault.deposit(100_000e6);
+
+        vm.prank(owner);
+        vault.pause();
+
+        vm.prank(alice);
+        vault.withdraw(shares);
+    }
+
+    /// @notice Worse: `Ownable.renounceOwnership` is inherited and not disabled. Pause
+    ///         then renounce -- deliberately or by a fat-fingered ops script -- and
+    ///         every depositor's USDT is locked in the contract forever.
+    function test_FundsCannotBeLockedForeverByPauseThenRenounce() public {
+        vm.prank(alice);
+        uint256 shares = vault.deposit(100_000e6);
+
+        vm.startPrank(owner);
+        vault.pause();
+        vault.renounceOwnership();
+        vm.stopPrank();
+
+        assertEq(vault.owner(), address(0), "ownership is gone");
+        console.log("vault is paused    :", vault.paused());
+        console.log("vault owner        :", vault.owner());
+        console.log("alice's locked USDT:", vault.balanceOfUnderlying(alice));
+
+        // Nobody can ever unpause again, so this must still work. It does not.
+        vm.prank(alice);
+        vault.withdraw(shares);
+    }
+}
diff --git a/test/FeeAccounting.t.sol b/test/FeeAccounting.t.sol
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..0ca1875
--- /dev/null
+++ b/test/FeeAccounting.t.sol
@@ -0,0 +1,150 @@
+// SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT
+pragma solidity 0.8.28;
+
+import {Test, console} from "forge-std/Test.sol";
+
+import {UsdtYieldVault} from "../src/UsdtYieldVault.sol";
+import {MockUSDT} from "./mocks/MockUSDT.sol";
+import {MockAavePool, MockAToken} from "./mocks/MockAavePool.sol";
+
+/// @notice Checks the fee behaviour the contract's own NatSpec promises:
+///         "Both fees stay supplied in Aave and accrue to the remaining shareholders
+///          rather than being swept to the owner."  (src/UsdtYieldVault.sol:17-18)
+///         Uses the team's own non-yielding mock, so nothing here depends on my harness.
+contract FeeAccountingTest is Test {
+    UsdtYieldVault vault;
+    MockUSDT usdt;
+    MockAavePool pool;
+    MockAToken aUsdt;
+
+    address owner = makeAddr("owner");
+    address alice = makeAddr("alice");
+    address bob = makeAddr("bob");
+
+    uint256 constant DEPOSIT = 100_000e6;
+
+    function setUp() public {
+        usdt = new MockUSDT();
+        pool = new MockAavePool(address(usdt));
+        aUsdt = pool.aToken();
+        vault = new UsdtYieldVault(address(usdt), address(aUsdt), address(pool), 10, owner);
+
+        usdt.mint(alice, 1_000_000e6);
+        usdt.mint(bob, 1_000_000e6);
+        vm.prank(alice);
+        usdt.approve(address(vault), type(uint256).max);
+        vm.prank(bob);
+        usdt.approve(address(vault), type(uint256).max);
+    }
+
+    /*//////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////
+        W-1: the withdrawal fee reaches nobody
+    //////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////*/
+
+    /// @notice Bob pays a 0.3% exit fee. Per the NatSpec that fee stays in Aave and
+    ///         accrues to the remaining shareholders -- here, Alice.
+    function test_WithdrawFeeAccruesToRemainingShareholders() public {
+        vm.prank(alice);
+        vault.deposit(DEPOSIT);
+        vm.prank(bob);
+        uint256 bobShares = vault.deposit(DEPOSIT);
+
+        uint256 aliceBefore = vault.balanceOfUnderlying(alice);
+        uint256 bobGross = vault.convertToAssets(bobShares);
+        uint256 exitFee = (bobGross * vault.WITHDRAW_FEE_BPS()) / vault.BPS_DENOMINATOR();
+
+        vm.prank(bob);
+        uint256 bobGot = vault.withdraw(bobShares);
+
+        uint256 aliceAfter = vault.balanceOfUnderlying(alice);
+
+        console.log("bob's gross redemption            :", bobGross);
+        console.log("exit fee bob paid                 :", exitFee);
+        console.log("alice underlying before bob exits :", aliceBefore);
+        console.log("alice underlying after  bob exits :", aliceAfter);
+        console.log("alice actually gained             :", aliceAfter - aliceBefore);
+        console.log("bob received                      :", bobGot);
+
+        assertApproxEqAbs(aliceAfter - aliceBefore, exitFee, 1, "exit fee should accrue to the remaining shareholder");
+    }
+
+    /// @notice `withdraw` debits `totalAssetsStored` by the GROSS amount but only pulls
+    ///         the NET amount out of Aave (src/UsdtYieldVault.sol:117-119). The fee is
+    ///         therefore dropped from the vault's books entirely.
+    function test_BooksMatchTheAavePosition() public {
+        vm.prank(alice);
+        vault.deposit(DEPOSIT);
+        vm.prank(bob);
+        uint256 bobShares = vault.deposit(DEPOSIT);
+
+        vm.prank(bob);
+        vault.withdraw(bobShares);
+
+        console.log("vault.totalAssetsStored() :", vault.totalAssetsStored());
+        console.log("aUSDT actually held       :", aUsdt.balanceOf(address(vault)));
+        console.log("untracked drift           :", aUsdt.balanceOf(address(vault)) - vault.totalAssetsStored());
+
+        assertEq(
+            vault.totalAssetsStored(),
+            aUsdt.balanceOf(address(vault)),
+            "vault books must match its actual Aave position"
+        );
+    }
+
+    /// @notice Every exit fee ever paid is permanently orphaned: it is neither
+    ///         redistributed nor withdrawable by the owner.
+    function test_ExitFeesAreNotOrphanedAfterEveryoneLeaves() public {
+        vm.prank(alice);
+        uint256 aShares = vault.deposit(DEPOSIT);
+        vm.prank(bob);
+        uint256 bShares = vault.deposit(DEPOSIT);
+
+        vm.prank(alice);
+        vault.withdraw(aShares);
+        vm.prank(bob);
+        vault.withdraw(bShares);
+
+        uint256 orphaned = aUsdt.balanceOf(address(vault));
+        console.log("total deposited      :", 2 * DEPOSIT);
+        console.log("shares outstanding   :", vault.totalShares());
+        console.log("orphaned aUSDT       :", orphaned);
+
+        assertEq(orphaned, 0, "no fee may be orphaned once all shares are burned");
+    }
+
+    /*//////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////
+        W-2: characterising the deposit fee (these PASS -- documenting,
+             not alleging, so the numbers are on the record)
+    //////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////*/
+
+    /// @notice The first depositor is charged a fee that is credited straight back to
+    ///         her, because she owns 100% of the shares the fee accrues to.
+    function test_Characterise_FirstDepositorPaysNoEffectiveFee() public {
+        vm.prank(alice);
+        vault.deposit(DEPOSIT);
+
+        console.log("nominal 0.1% deposit fee :", (DEPOSIT * 10) / 10_000);
+        console.log("alice underlying         :", vault.balanceOfUnderlying(alice));
+
+        assertEq(vault.balanceOfUnderlying(alice), DEPOSIT, "sole depositor keeps the whole deposit");
+    }
+
+    /// @notice The "deposit fee" is not a protocol fee: it is an instant transfer from
+    ///         the new depositor to whoever already holds shares. Bob books a loss the
+    ///         moment his deposit lands. This is what makes F-5 (owner fee spike) pay.
+    function test_Characterise_DepositFeeIsPaidToIncumbentHolders() public {
+        vm.prank(alice);
+        vault.deposit(DEPOSIT);
+        uint256 aliceBefore = vault.balanceOfUnderlying(alice);
+
+        vm.prank(bob);
+        vault.deposit(DEPOSIT);
+
+        console.log("bob deposited                       :", DEPOSIT);
+        console.log("bob underlying immediately after    :", vault.balanceOfUnderlying(bob));
+        console.log("bob's instant loss                  :", DEPOSIT - vault.balanceOfUnderlying(bob));
+        console.log("alice gained from bob's deposit fee :", vault.balanceOfUnderlying(alice) - aliceBefore);
+
+        assertLt(vault.balanceOfUnderlying(bob), DEPOSIT, "new depositor immediately owns less than he paid in");
+    }
+}
